WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.034
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:04.370
hosts the Emmy-
and Peabody Award-winning show

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.739
"Last Week Tonight,"
which airs Sunday nights on HBO

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:08.141
and streams on HBO Max.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.209
Please welcome back to the show
our good friend

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:11.678
John Oliver, everybody!

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.179
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:23.189 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.625 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.627
How are you?
-Good, thank you.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.629
Thank you.
Thank you.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:30.796
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thanks.

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:33.299
No, no.
-You love this stuff.

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.668
-I d--
-You love applause

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.603
and attention.
-I don't like it.

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:39.038
I don't like it.
[ Cheering ]

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:41.340
I was this close to leaving.
Half is a bit.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.275
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:45.177
-I feel very lucky
to see you here.

00:00:45.177 --> 00:00:46.912
I saw you last week
at the Emmys.

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:48.881
Congratulations on your victory.
-Thanks very much.

00:00:48.881 --> 00:00:51.717
-Also, congratulations --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:53.753
I feel like you had --

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.189
The award was presented to you
by Selena Gomez,

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:57.457
Martin Short, and Steve Martin.
-Yes.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:58.857
-That's a very nice trio
to get it from.

00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:01.094
-Definitely, yeah.
I kind of -- when they came out,

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:03.529
they'd said,
"Our award is next,"

00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:05.865
and it did seem like
this must be a mistake.

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:09.001
They're not gonna slum it in
"Best Variety Show," are they?

00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:10.302
And then they did.
Yeah, it was amazing.

00:01:10.302 --> 00:01:12.138
My wife's favorite movie
is "Clifford."

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.106
-Yeah.
-So to get an award

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:15.808
from Clifford is about as happy

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:17.510
as she's been with me
for five years.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.979
-Based on the reaction
to you saying that,

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.414
I think it might only
be her favorite movie.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:22.415
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:23.516
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.285
'Cause I --
that's a Martin Short film

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:26.919
where Martin Short
plays a child named Clifford.

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:28.687
-Yeah, it's really good.
It's really good.

00:01:28.687 --> 00:01:31.690
-It's really good.
-And even though we're both

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.026
selling it really hard,
you can feel an innate sense of,

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:35.628
"I don't think --"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.897
There's no curiosity of,
"I'll watch that tonight."

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.932
It's just, "Nope, this is
another thing that I will miss."

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:42.535
-Just do us a favor.
It's not a giant commitment.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.204
Watch the trailer
on YouTube of "Clifford"...

00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:46.572
-Yeah.
-...starring Martin Short.

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:48.107
-And then, when you get to
the end of it and think,

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:50.677
"Not for me," remember,
it is really good.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:51.944
-Yep.
-To be honest,

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:53.446
I've just come here to sell
"Clifford."

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:54.647
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:55.948
I just don't think --
-This is a new thing.

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:57.883
You're plugging old films.
-Yes, that's right.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:59.985
I just don't think enough people
have seen "Clifford."

00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:03.422
So, do we have a clip?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:05.825
-You got to tell us ahead
of time if you want a clip.

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:07.292
I'm so sorry.
-Oh, that's how it works?

00:02:07.292 --> 00:02:09.028
I thought you could just say,
"Do you have a clip?",

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:10.964
and they'd google it quick.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.398
-Give them time.
Maybe by the end.

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:14.300
-Yeah, sure.
-So what about --

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.101
Do you enjoy being at the Emmys?
-No.

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:17.270
-Yeah.
-Not at all, no.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.671
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.105
No, it would've been nicer
to see you

00:02:20.105 --> 00:02:21.607
literally anywhere else
on Earth.

00:02:21.607 --> 00:02:23.776
But, you know, it's what
America does best, in a way.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.244
It's pageantry.
-Yeah.

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:28.681
-It's like a royal funeral
without a dead queen.

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:30.883
-Okay. Well...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:33.752
You know what I like about you?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.752 --> 00:02:34.954
You know what I like
the most about you?

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:37.256
You bring your own segues.
Now, John...

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:38.758
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.626
The last time you were here
was a couple days

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:42.461
after Prince Philip
had passed away.

00:02:42.461 --> 00:02:43.863
-That's right, yes.
-And now, obviously you're here

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.266
on the day
of the Queen's funeral.

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:47.767
-I am, yeah.
-And you're sort of our royal

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.401
correspondent by fate
more than anything else.

00:02:49.401 --> 00:02:50.937
-That's right, that's right.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:54.607
-How are you taking it all in?
-Thanks, thanks.

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.911
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.911 --> 00:02:59.845
Yeah, it's a difficult day.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:01.380
Craig, you're from
the U.K., right?

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.483
-I am, yeah.
-Sorry for our loss.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:04.583
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.583 --> 00:03:06.119
-You know what?
I didn't know her.

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:08.555
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:09.622
-Wait.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:13.860
You guys promised me
Craig knew her.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.530
-Yeah, you know, basically,
whenever a senior royal dies,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.866
I will come here.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.368
Just click your heels
three times, and I will be here

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.103
to give it
the disrespect it deserves.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.138
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:27.273
Well, thank you very much.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.309
-It's been a very weird time
in Britain.

00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:30.709
This 10 days in forced mourning,

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.679
apparently,
has been very, very strange.

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:34.079
-Yes.
And one of the --

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:35.681
We were talking about this
at the Emmys,

00:03:35.681 --> 00:03:38.084
'cause we're both football fans.
-Yes.

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.752
-I should clarify --
we're talking about soccer.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.387
Which was --
they did not play any

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.923
of those games for about a week.
-They didn't.

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:47.593
They kept -- rugby and cricket
was allowed to go ahead.

00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:50.028
[ Laughter ]
And football had to stop

00:03:50.028 --> 00:03:52.732
in an act of, I think,
tangible class warfare.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.367
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:56.502
-Because of the fan bases
of those three sports?

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.570
-I mean, it's hard to --

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.138
They will say
it was for other reasons,

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:00.706
but I think
it was probably that.

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:02.008
-Yeah.
-I think they were concerned

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:03.843
about how football fans
would respond

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:05.911
to a minute's silence
and maybe they would not

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:07.013
be able to go the whole minute.

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:08.947
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.947 --> 00:04:10.583
But it's the interesting thing.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.218
It's always -- the question
in their mind is always,

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:15.421
"Well, will they behave?",
not "Why might they not?"

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:16.556
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:18.791
There's no introspection
or follow-up

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:21.593
for, "Why might they decide
to editorialize that?"

00:04:21.593 --> 00:04:24.197
-Yeah, and then,
there's also the likelihood

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:27.533
of maybe a chant or two.
Or a song that could happen.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:30.002
-That's right.
We do love a song, yeah.

00:04:30.002 --> 00:04:31.470
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.539
Yeah, it's been
a very weird 10 days.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:37.476
I think -- they had in
Morrison's, the supermarket --

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:39.112
#Ad.
Sorry.

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:43.282
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:46.252
I'm here -- I'm here to promote
"Clifford" the movie

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:49.322
and a low-grade
British supermarket.

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.524
[ Laughter ]
Apparently, they lowered

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:53.792
the sound of the beeps
at the checkout

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:55.862
out of respect
for the queen dying.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.597
[ Laughter ]
-Well, she did --

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.432
She hated beeps.
-She hated beeps.

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.833
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.002
As if someone would've gone in,
heard the regular beep,

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:05.071
and said,
"Have some [bleep] respect!"

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:06.406
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:09.942
"She's lying in state!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:12.979
"As is tradition,
a quieter beep is appropriate!"

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.747
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.215
-I can't believe there was a way

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:18.151
to have the beep be quieter
this whole time.

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.085
-Well, at first,
I thought that cannot be true.

00:05:20.085 --> 00:05:22.154
They can mute the beeps.
They choose not to.

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:23.355
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:23.355 --> 00:05:25.224
-Again, that's the crime here.
-That's right.

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:28.394
And after Charles becomes king,
the beeps will return.

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:29.595
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:30.629
-Double, twice as loud.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:32.965
-Unnecessarily loud volume,
yeah.

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:36.536
It's been a stupid time.
-You were offered

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:39.539
the order of the British Empire,
the O.B.E., I guess.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:41.274
-I was, yes.

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:43.676
-And that must be a very
interesting call to receive.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:45.912
-It is.
It is a call.

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:47.245
They called up, and they --

00:05:47.245 --> 00:05:49.382
This is before I got
my citizenship here in America.

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:51.851
And so, you know,
as an immigrant on a visa

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.719
or green card,
your immigration status

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:56.556
is a perpetual anxiety for you.

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:59.224
They left a message saying
that you need to call

00:05:59.224 --> 00:06:00.927
the British consulate
immediately,

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:02.094
which doesn't sound like --

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:03.996
Unless you're Jason Bourne,
and...

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:05.197
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.197 --> 00:06:07.834
...physically and emotionally,
I just am not.

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.835
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.835 --> 00:06:11.504
I thought it was something
really bad.

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:13.539
And then, this guy picks up
the phone and says,

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:18.010
"I'm here with a letter
from the Queen in front of me."

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:21.180
It was so offensively English,
his accent.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.414
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:25.183
I don't think you're allowed
to do this accent.

00:06:25.183 --> 00:06:27.219
Yeah, and he said,
"I'm here to say

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:29.455
that you're on the New Year's
honors list,"

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.690
and I knew what was coming.
"I don't think I want this."

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.059
He went, "Don't answer now."
And I said, "I just did."

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:35.127
[ Laughter ]
I said --

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:36.395
I said, "I don't want it."

00:06:36.395 --> 00:06:39.231
And so he said,
"I'll call back tomorrow."

00:06:39.231 --> 00:06:41.567
And he did. I said, "Yeah,
my answer hasn't changed.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.069
I really don't want this."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:47.140
It's a loaded --
O.B.E....

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.340
-Yeah.
-...is a loa--

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:51.310
The "B.E." part of that
is a hell of a thing

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.579
to want after your surname.
Yeah, so I didn't.

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:55.781
And he said,
"Please don't tell anyone,"

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:57.783
and I said,
"I am telling everybody I know."

00:06:57.783 --> 00:06:59.117
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:59.117 --> 00:07:01.553
That is --
Let's just be clear.

00:07:01.553 --> 00:07:02.854
I have made no pr--

00:07:02.854 --> 00:07:05.324
If I'm making a promise,
I'm breaking it instantly.

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:07.760
The moment this phone goes down,
I'm calling people.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.129
-I bet -- obviously,
based on his reaction,

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:12.598
it probably doesn't happen often
that people turn him down.

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.667
-I think -- I think it probably
happens all the time.

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:16.468
I just think it's not then
people that just say,

00:07:16.468 --> 00:07:19.838
"I have to tell people that this
was a very funny conversation."

00:07:19.838 --> 00:07:21.707
-You had --
-My wife was mad, though,

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:22.875
'cause she wanted to go
to Buckingham Palace.

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:24.176
I had to kind of explain to her,

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:26.946
"Listen, there's a class system
in England.

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:29.281
This is stuff -- it's not
important for you to know this,

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:31.584
but I really can't go there
and behave."

00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:33.252
[ Laughter ]

00:07:33.252 --> 00:07:35.387
-You did have a joke
censored by --

00:07:35.387 --> 00:07:36.388
-Yes!
-I guess your show airs

00:07:36.388 --> 00:07:37.856
on Sky over there.
-Yes.

00:07:37.856 --> 00:07:41.794
Yeah, they, uh --
-Not really a censorable joke.

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.362
-It was -- I don't --
It wasn't really --

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:44.596
It barely had the properties
of a --

00:07:44.596 --> 00:07:47.833
All we said was
that Britain is still mourning

00:07:47.833 --> 00:07:50.669
the shocking loss -- right? --
of a 96-year-old woman

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:52.037
from natural causes.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:52.037 --> 00:07:54.206
It's not -- it's not --
literally not a joke.

00:07:54.206 --> 00:07:55.540
Scientifically,
that's not a joke.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.411
That's just a fact with a kind
of dickish inflection.

00:07:58.411 --> 00:07:59.712
That's all it is.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:01.580
And yet, they cut it out.
-Wow.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.783
-Which is pretty [bleep]
because apparently

00:08:03.783 --> 00:08:05.016
all we've heard all week
is the Queen

00:08:05.016 --> 00:08:06.585
had an incredible
sense of humor.

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:08.187
[ Laughter ]

00:08:08.187 --> 00:08:10.556
Just non-stop,
"Oh, she was so funny."

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.891
Seth, she was so funny.

00:08:12.891 --> 00:08:15.661
The Queen, she was razor sharp.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.763
She dreamt of going
to the Del Close Marathon.

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:19.698
Big comedy fan.
-I will say,

00:08:19.698 --> 00:08:22.001
I've watched a lot of anecdotes
of people saying,

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:24.169
"She was so funny,"
and then they tell a story,

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:26.405
and when it ends, you go,
"I don't know."

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:28.708
-Yeah, or that clip.
Or that clip.

00:08:28.708 --> 00:08:32.211
It's like -- And there she was
with Paddington.

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:34.613
I'm watching a bear carry her
comedically in that scene.

00:08:34.613 --> 00:08:35.848
[ Laughter ]

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:38.184
She's giving that bear
nothing back.

00:08:38.184 --> 00:08:41.354
There is one set of footprints
in the sand, and it's a paw.

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:44.056
[ Laughter ]

00:08:44.056 --> 00:08:47.159
But she was a huge comedy fan.
-Huge comedy fan.

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:49.027
And liked the good --
yeah, "The State."

00:08:49.027 --> 00:08:51.831
She loved "The State."
[ Laughter ]

00:08:51.831 --> 00:08:54.066
-She just wanted to live to see
Mulaney's tour.

00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:55.668
That was the thing
that she didn't --

00:08:55.668 --> 00:08:56.936
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:59.105
Why is that the thing?!

00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:00.939
Ask yourself
why that was your line.

00:09:00.939 --> 00:09:03.442
-Yeah, and then let us know.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:05.144
John Oliver, everybody!

00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:06.312
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:08.447
"Last Week Tonight"
airs Sunday nights on HBO

00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:09.581
and streams on HBO Max.

00:09:09.581 --> 00:09:11.350
We'll be right back
with Quinta Brunson.

